      "lineNbr": 406,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-15T17:02:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Does the service check that the IP addresses are valid?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 406,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 406,
        "endChar": 36
      },
      "revId": "9bd3bf930e4516ab66f5758f3cf3850595cd1a31",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "24481289_013eb2d5",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/IpSecTransform.java",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 406,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-16T00:37:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It can be anything but empty/null. If it\u0027s not a valid address then it just won\u0027t work for anything. But, as you pointed out, addresses are inherently racy, so this is a caveat emptor option that will further tighten up the user\u0027s transform. For instance, if the outbound SA is created with source address A but the socket they open ultimately binds to address B, then traffic will fail, which will tell them to either re-bind the socket or re-negotiate the SA. But, from testing it seems that not specifying it is fine. I\u0027m worried that forcing it to be specified will have corner-case issues in IPv6. let\u0027s discuss when you have time?",
      "parentUuid": "df889b5b_9105741b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 406,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 406,
        "endChar": 36
      },
      "revId": "9bd3bf930e4516ab66f5758f3cf3850595cd1a31",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
